---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="The M5 Neural Accelerator"
  description="Apple's per-GPU-core matrix engine speeds up prefill 3-4x but barely moves decode. What that means for local-LLM benchmarks, and how we confirmed the NA-aware MLX engine serves our runs."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Lab · Hardware</p>
    <h1>The M5 Neural Accelerator</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Apple put a dedicated matrix engine in every M5 GPU core. The speedup is real &mdash;
      and lopsided. This is our running record of what it actually does to local-LLM inference
      numbers, and how it shapes the way we read our own.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="lab-status">Ongoing investigation &middot; started 2026-08-22 &middot; lab notebook &mdash; dated sections, updated as the picture changes</p>

  <section class="lab-section">
    <h2>What it is <span class="sec-date">2026-08-22</span></h2>
    <p>
      With the M5 generation (M5, M5 Pro, M5 Max), Apple redesigned the GPU so that
      <strong>every core carries a dedicated Neural Accelerator</strong> &mdash; an on-die
      matrix engine for the dense matmuls transformer inference lives on, analogous to a
      discrete GPU&rsquo;s tensor cores. Prior Apple GPUs ran matmul on general-purpose SIMD
      units; this is new, purpose-built silicon.
    </p>
    <p>
      It&rsquo;s exposed to developers through <strong>Metal 4&rsquo;s Tensor Operations
      (TensorOps)</strong> and Metal Performance Primitives, and Apple updated
      <strong>MLX</strong> &mdash; their own array framework &mdash; to use these directly.
      One footnote matters operationally: MLX only engages the Neural Accelerators on
      <strong>macOS 26.2 or later</strong>. This is a framework-level capability, not any one
      app&rsquo;s trick &mdash; anything built on a recent-enough MLX, running on 26.2+,
      should benefit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section">
    <h2>The speedup is lopsided &mdash; and that&rsquo;s the whole story <span class="sec-date">2026-08-22</span></h2>
    <p>
      Apple&rsquo;s own benchmark (base M5 vs M4) shows the Neural Accelerator&rsquo;s benefit
      is <strong>almost entirely a prefill/time-to-first-token win, not a decode-throughput
      win</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="lab-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Speedup (M5 vs M4)</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Time-to-first-token (TTFT)</td>
            <td class="num">3.3&times; &ndash; 4.06&times;</td>
            <td>Prefill is compute-bound &mdash; exactly what a matmul accelerator helps.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Generation speed (tok/s)</td>
            <td class="num">1.19&times; &ndash; 1.27&times;</td>
            <td>Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound &mdash; a matmul engine
            can&rsquo;t fix a bandwidth ceiling. The 19&ndash;27% gain tracks almost exactly
            with M5&rsquo;s 28% memory-bandwidth increase over M4 (153 vs 120&nbsp;GB/s),
            suggesting bandwidth, not the accelerator, is doing most of that work.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      That attribution isn&rsquo;t just ours: an independent guide reached the same conclusion
      separately &mdash; decode gains on M5 &ldquo;track almost linearly with the higher memory
      bandwidth.&rdquo; Two independent sources landing on the same explanation is a good
      accuracy signal.
    </p>
    <p class="callout-inline">
      <strong>The implication for benchmarking:</strong> if you want to see the Neural
      Accelerator&rsquo;s real effect, <strong>TTFT is the metric to watch, not tok/s</strong>.
      Tok/s-centric comparisons &mdash; which is most of what the local-LLM community publishes
      &mdash; are looking mostly at the metric the NA barely moves. Our own throughput axis
      is measured, and this finding is why we treat prefill and decode as separate stories
      rather than folding them into one speed number.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section">
    <h2>Confirmed: our MLX runs go through the NA-aware engine <span class="sec-date">2026-08-22</span></h2>
    <p>
      The bench host is an Apple M5 Max running LM Studio, on a macOS build above the 26.2
      floor. LM Studio ships two separate MLX runtime packages: a generic MLX build, and an
      <strong>&ldquo;Apple M5&rdquo; build with explicit M5 Neural Accelerator support</strong>.
      We verified in the runtime manager that the M5-specific build is the one selected and
      active for the MLX slot &mdash; meaning <strong>every MLX-format run this bench has
      published was served through the NA-aware engine</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A second, independent confirmation comes from the engine&rsquo;s own internal naming.
      The runtime listing identifies the selected MLX engine as
      <code>mlx-llm-mac-arm64-apple-metal-<strong>nax</strong>-advsimd@1.11.0</code> &mdash;
      the <code>nax</code> segment (almost certainly &ldquo;Neural Accelerator
      eXtension&rdquo; or similar) versus the plain
      <code>mlx-llm-mac-arm64-apple-metal-advsimd</code> variant that isn&rsquo;t selected.
      The NA-aware build is active in the engine&rsquo;s own package name, not just a GUI label.
    </p>
    <p>
      There&rsquo;s a structural reason this works without special effort on LM Studio&rsquo;s
      part: their open-source MLX engine is a thin wrapper directly around Apple&rsquo;s own
      <code>mlx-lm</code> package, built by the MLX team itself. NA support landed inside
      MLX/<code>mlx-lm</code>, so the wrapper inherits it whenever the pinned dependency is
      bumped &mdash; no from-scratch reimplementation involved.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section">
    <h2>Caveats we&rsquo;re carrying <span class="sec-date">2026-08-22</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple&rsquo;s numbers are base-M5.</strong> Their benchmark used a base M5
        (24&nbsp;GB) against M4. The <em>relative</em> TTFT-vs-decode pattern should hold
        architecturally on M5 Pro/Max, but the exact magnitudes on M5 Max &mdash; a much
        larger GPU with higher bandwidth &mdash; are untested by Apple&rsquo;s post and
        unconfirmed by us.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Our historical TTFT data can&rsquo;t answer this retroactively.</strong>
        Apple&rsquo;s methodology fixed the prompt at 4,096 tokens; our archived runs vary
        prompt length freely, so their TTFT samples swing with prompt size and can&rsquo;t be
        read as an NA signal. A controlled fixed-prompt TTFT baseline is on the workbench.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Metric weighting is an open question.</strong> If TTFT is where the
        hardware&rsquo;s win actually shows up, runtime-and-hardware comparisons that lean on
        tok/s may under-credit (or miscredit) effects across the board. We&rsquo;re deciding
        how much weight TTFT deserves in future runtime-comparison writeups.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="src-list">
      <li><a href="https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/exploring-llms-mlx-m5" rel="noopener">Apple ML Research: Exploring LLMs with MLX and the Neural Accelerators in the M5 GPU</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2025/10/apple-unleashes-m5-the-next-big-leap-in-ai-performance-for-apple-silicon/" rel="noopener">Apple Newsroom: M5 announcement</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/03/apple-debuts-m5-pro-and-m5-max-to-supercharge-the-most-demanding-pro-workflows/" rel="noopener">Apple Newsroom: M5 Pro / M5 Max debut</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19438v1" rel="noopener">BaseRT: Advancing Best-in-Class LLM Inference with Apple M5 Neural Accelerators (arXiv)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://ollama.com/blog/mlx-performance" rel="noopener">Ollama blog: MLX performance / NVFP4</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="lab-back"><a href="/lab/">&larr; Back to the Lab</a></p>
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-status {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 18px 0 0;
  }
  .lab-status::before { content: '\25B8 '; color: var(--green); }

  .lab-section { margin: 40px 0 0; max-width: 820px; }
  .lab-section h2 {
    font-size: 21px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    padding-top: 12px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
  }
  .sec-date {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-left: 10px;
    vertical-align: 2px;
  }
  .lab-section p, .lab-section li { color: var(--text-dim); }
  .lab-section ul { padding-left: 22px; }
  .lab-section li { margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .lab-section code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.86em;
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1px 6px;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .callout-inline {
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid rgba(37, 99, 235, 0.45);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 14px 18px;
  }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 18px 0; }
  .lab-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 14px;
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .lab-table th {
    text-align: left;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .lab-table td {
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    color: var(--text-dim);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .lab-table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
  .lab-table td.num {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    color: var(--green);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .src-list { padding-left: 22px; }
  .lab-back { margin: 40px 0 40px; }
</style>
